<section id="integration-forum">
<h3>Forum <span class="badge bg-secondary">mod_forum</span></h3>

<p>Moodle forums carry <strong>two independent grading systems</strong>, and the
grader supports both. Which one is in force is read from the forum's own
settings, with whole-forum grading taking precedence if somebody has configured
both:</p>

<table class="table table-sm">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Turned on by</th><th>Where the grade lives</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Whole forum</strong></td>
            <td>Whole forum grading &rarr; Type is not <em>None</em></td>
            <td><code>forum_grades</code>, gradebook <code>itemnumber 1</code>. One grade you type.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Ratings</strong></td>
            <td>Ratings &rarr; Aggregate type is not <em>No ratings</em>, with a scale set</td>
            <td><code>rating</code>, gradebook <code>itemnumber 0</code>. Computed by Moodle from every rating on every post.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Feedback only</strong></td>
            <td>Neither of the above</td>
            <td>No grade — a <em>Mark as graded</em> toggle and written feedback.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h4>Preview: three ways to read the posts</h4>
<p>Choose with the <em>Post view</em> switch above the preview. The choice is
remembered per teacher, per forum. Rated forums open on <em>In context</em>,
since the post is the unit being graded; other forums open on <em>All posts</em>.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>All posts</strong> — this student's posts, grouped by discussion, oldest first. Also exactly what the student and the feedback PDF see.</li>
    <li><strong>In context</strong> — one post at a time with the discussion prompt, the post it replied to, the other replies to that same post, and the replies it drew. Prev/next steps through the student's posts. The sibling replies are the useful part: they are how you tell whether a contribution added something or restated a classmate.</li>
    <li><strong>Thread</strong> — the whole discussion in reading order with this student's posts highlighted; long runs of unrelated posts fold away. Best for judging originality against what was actually said.</li>
</ul>
<p>Clicking a post in the context views, or a row in the marking panel's ratings
list, moves both panels to the same post — they stay in step. The context views
are for markers only: a student's feedback page shows their own posts alone.</p>

<h4>Preview: everything else</h4>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Embedded media</strong> (video, audio, images) are rewritten through Moodle's media filter so they play inline — no broken <code>@@PLUGINFILE@@</code> URLs.</li>
    <li><strong>Wordcount badge</strong> on each post header.</li>
    <li><strong>Plagiarism shield</strong> appears next to each post when a plagiarism plugin (Copyleaks, Turnitin, etc.) is configured. Per-attachment results are additionally listed in the marking-panel <em>Plagiarism</em> card — the same card assignments use (see <a href="#plugin-plagiarism">Plagiarism plugins</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Attachments</strong> render in the right-rail file selector exactly like assignment attachments.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Marking</h4>
<ul>
    <li>Whole-forum grades are written to <code>forum_grades</code> with <code>itemnumber = 1</code>.</li>
    <li>Forum has no native feedback table; comments are stored in the gradebook's <code>grade_grades.feedback</code>, with file attachments living in a plugin-owned filearea (<code>local_unifiedgrader/forumfeedback</code>).</li>
    <li>The <em>"Mark as graded"</em> toggle replaces the grade input for forums with grade type <em>None</em>.</li>
    <li>The <code>-</code> / <code>--</code> grade-clearing shortcuts apply here too — a light reset nulls the <code>forum_grades</code> row, a deliberate reset deletes it and lifts a stuck gradebook override (see <a href="#feature-grades">Grade input, clearing &amp; resets</a>). The student's posts are never touched.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Marking a rated forum</h4>
<p>Ratings work differently enough to be worth spelling out. The grade is not
something you type — Moodle computes it by aggregating every rating on every
post the student wrote, using the forum's own <em>Aggregate type</em>
(average, sum, count, maximum or minimum).</p>
<ul>
    <li>The grade box and any rubric or marking guide are hidden. In their place is a <strong>Post ratings</strong> list — one row per post, with a running total showing what the gradebook will get.</li>
    <li>Each row shows <strong>two different numbers</strong>: the dropdown holds <em>your</em> rating, while the figure beside it is the aggregate across everyone who has rated that post. A colleague marking the same forum moves the aggregate without touching your choice.</li>
    <li>Choosing <em>Rate…</em> withdraws your rating rather than scoring it zero. If that was the last rating on the student's work, the gradebook cell is cleared too.</li>
    <li>A student counts as graded only once <strong>every</strong> post they wrote has been rated by somebody, so a half-marked student stays under <em>Needs grading</em>.</li>
    <li>You cannot rate your own posts — Moodle forbids it, and those rows are disabled with the reason shown.</li>
    <li>Feedback is per student, not per post: use the feedback box for an overall comment. Moodle has no field for a comment on an individual rating.</li>
    <li>A deliberate reset withdraws <em>your</em> ratings from that student's posts. Another marker's ratings are left alone, and the posts themselves are never touched.</li>
</ul>
<p class="text-muted"><strong>Penalties and extensions are hidden on rated
forums.</strong> The gradebook value belongs to Moodle and is recomputed from
the ratings on every change, so a deduction would either be overwritten by the
next rating or stick as an override that quietly stops matching the ratings it
claims to represent.</p>

<h4>Forum extensions</h4>
<p>Moodle core forums don't have a native extension mechanism — the plugin
adds one via the <code>local_unifiedgrader_fext</code> table. Extensions are
respected when computing late status and when applying late penalties.</p>

<h4>Late penalty integration</h4>
<p>If the <code>gradepenalty_duedate</code> plugin is installed, late penalty
percentages are applied automatically using its configured rules. The raw
grade you enter is preserved in <code>forum_grades</code>; the penalised
grade is what the gradebook sees.</p>

<div class="local-unifiedgrader-help-screenshot">
    <i class="fa fa-image" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <span>Screenshot: forum grading showing posts grouped by discussion in the
    preview pane, plagiarism shield badge visible, and marking panel on the right.</span>
</div>

<h4>Notes</h4>
<ul>
    <li>The plugin doesn't override Moodle's native <em>View grades</em> button on the <em>student</em> forum view; that flow uses a feedback banner injected via PSR-14 hook (see <a href="#feature-feedback">Rich feedback</a>).</li>
    <li>For forums with grade type <em>None</em>, the marking guide / rubric section is hidden — assigning criteria values would be a non-sequitur without a numeric grade.</li>
</ul>
</section>
